.TH OB-RECORD-SINK 8 "June 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-record-sink \- privileged session-recording sink
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-record-sink
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ob-record-sink
is the privileged half of the tamper-evident session-recording flow. It is
socket-activated by systemd (one short-lived instance per connection,
.BR ob-record.socket )
and is not run by hand. It runs as
.B root
so that the recordings it writes cannot be read, deleted or altered by the
recorded (unprivileged) user.
.PP
For each connection it:
.IP 1. 4
derives the recorded user from the connection's
.B SO_PEERCRED
(kernel-verified). This is the only source of identity \(em never the header \(em
so a caller can only ever record under its own name, and the
.RI < user >
path component is never client-controlled (no cross-user write, no path
traversal).
.IP 2. 4
reads a one-line JSON metadata header followed by the opaque recording stream
(typescript bytes) until EOF, sent by
.BR ob-record-connect (1).
.IP 3. 4
writes the recording and its metadata under
.I /var/lib/open-bastion/sessions/<user>/
\(em directory
.BR "root:ob-sessions 0750" ,
files
.BR 0640 .
The recording file is created with
.B O_NOFOLLOW
and
.B O_EXCL
(no symlink, no clobber); the JSON metadata is created with
.B O_NOFOLLOW
and rewritten
.RB ( O_TRUNC )
when the session is finalized.
.IP 4. 4
sends a one-byte acknowledgement to the connector once that metadata and
recording file exist, and only then reads the stream. Every rejection before
this point (oversized or malformed header, unsupported version, duplicate
session id, a directory it cannot create) closes the connection without the
ack, so
.BR ob-record-connect (1)
fails and the recorder refuses the session \(em recording can never be skipped
by a header the sink declines.
.PP
The sink records only what it observes: status
.B active
then
.B completed
(clean EOF),
.B truncated
(byte cap or duration cap reached) or
.B aborted
(abnormal drop, or the connecting process died while the connection was held
open by something else). The child command's exit code is deliberately not
recorded \(em it would be entirely client-reported.
.PP
Silence is not an abnormal end: an interactive session may print nothing for
hours, so the stream has no idle timeout. The sink instead watches the process
that connected (the
.B SO_PEERCRED
pid, through a pidfd when the kernel offers one, else
.BR kill (2)
with signal 0 at each idle wake-up), and bounds every recording by a 1 GiB
byte cap and a total duration cap. Only the header must arrive within 30
seconds of the connection.
.SH THREAT MODEL
Root is trusted; the sink defends only against the unprivileged user tampering
with its own recordings. Defending against root requires an LSM, append-only
media or remote log shipping, which are out of scope.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OB_SESSIONS_DIR
Override the base sessions directory (default
.IR /var/lib/open-bastion/sessions ).
Read from the daemon's own environment, which is set by the systemd unit (never
by the connecting client); intended for tests.
.TP
.B OB_RECORD_MAX_SEC
Total duration cap of one recording, in seconds (default 604800, seven days).
A recording that reaches it is finalized as
.BR truncated ,
and the session loses its recording channel. Set it with a drop-in
.RB ( "systemctl edit ob-record@.service" )
if sessions may legitimately last longer; it should not be shorter than the
recorder's
.BR max_duration .
.TP
.B OB_RECORD_POLL_SEC
How often, in seconds, an idle stream wakes the sink to check the peer and the
duration cap (default 30). The tests set it to 1.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/open-bastion/rec.sock
The session-recording socket
.RB ( ob-record.socket ).
.TP
.I /var/lib/open-bastion/sessions/<user>/
Recordings and metadata (root-owned).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ob-record-connect (1),
.BR ob-session-recorder (8),
.BR ob-cert-daemon (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Linagora
